 (4, "An archive paper of April 2026 holds that Sappho's poem \"is a love poem until Pseudo-Longinus reads it as an exemplar of the sublime, and then it becomes a theory of language itself\".", ["A625-01"]),
 (4, "The archive's fuller account, The Echo and the Stamp (#1458, August 2026), reads the treatise as a theory of how greatness crosses from the dead to the living and from the living to the unborn, with a mechanism stated twice in the technical vocabulary of ancient physics, resistance to erasure as its criterion, and a break in the middle of a return to the one source it never treated.", ["A1458-02"]),
 (4, "On that account the treatise defines the highest thing in literature as a reverberation and gives a silence as its supreme instance.", ["A1458-11"]),
 (4, "Its terms are read as one circuit: \"An emission, a reception, an impression, a residue, a retention, an inscription, an audience — and the audience becomes the next emitter.\"", ["A1458-12"]),
